paragraphs.text:
We will follow this idea through two applications of interference.
First, fringes reveal whether a surface is flat;
then, weakening reflected light allows more light to pass through a lens.

Wave $1$ and wave $2$ both undergo the same reversal, so the reflections themselves introduce no difference between their phase changes.
Here their relative phase depends on the extra distance travelled inside the film;
changing the thickness changes that relative phase.

The source selects green light from the middle of the spectrum, with vacuum wavelength $\lambda_{\text{绿}}=5.5\times10^{-5}$ centimetres.
For this green light at normal incidence, a thickness of $\frac{1}{4}$ of its wavelength inside the film cancels the reflection in the ideal case of equal reflected amplitudes;
nearly equal amplitudes leave a very weak reflection①.

In the transparent, non-absorbing ideal model used here, energy removed from reflection goes into transmission.
For the selected wavelength, complete cancellation of reflection with no other losses lets all incident energy pass forwards through the lens;
in a real instrument, absorption or other losses prevent us from concluding that weak reflection alone means complete transmission.

